{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789412118,"nonce":"NRYHELgGFFtXc8yHQC/9SIB+/WM=","message":"Stage 2 landed in two commits. d3e14b9: the Immix heap serves several\nthreads — side tables reserved once for the largest heap (no\ngrowth, no moving), chunk-aligned chunks with a shift-indexed chunk\nmap, the block pool behind a lock a refill takes, per-thread bump\nregions once the program is multithreaded (compiled code keeps the\nheap's own region while single-threaded), and the sweep leaves a held\nregion's block its free lines. The one measurable cost was an atomic\nRMW per allocation on the droppable-line bit; a byte per line stored\nplainly removed it (binary_trees back to parity on the NUC).\n\nSecond commit: stop-the-world (src/runtime/stw.rs). Per-view\nThreadState (running/safe, sp, stack top, krio fiber id, published\nroots); World with the request flag, a collector lock and a gate\ncondvar. Safepoints (interpreter poll, finish_alloc, poll_gc) answer\nthe request by parking; Sched::idle, Time.sleep and the outermost\ninterpret's return are safe regions. The collector gathers other\nthreads' published roots and walks their stacks and fiber chains\nfrom their published sp. VM::spawn_thread runs a body on a new\nthread with its own view and flips the heap to multithreaded. The\nintern table and the fiber list lock only once threaded.\n\nA two-thread allocate-and-collect test passes (e2e) and the suites,\ncorpus and site are unchanged; NUC numbers unchanged.\n\nThe test also showed where stage 3 starts: two threads in the\nintern table corrupted it before the lock. Stage 3 is next — the\ninline caches, method cache, method tables and tier installs — then\nthe pool and Fiber.go. The epoch-word poll at compiled loop headers\nis still open: a compiled loop with no allocation does not park.","files":null}]}